    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This study delves into the activities and substrate specificities of Porcine Circovirus Type 2 (PCV2) endonucleases, shedding light on their structural and functional characteristics. Through a targeted approach, we expressed and purified both wild-type PCV2 and a catalytically inactive mutant (dPCV), facilitating a comparative analysis of their reactivities with single-stranded DNA (ssDNA). Our findings reveal that while PCV2 adeptly cleaves increasing concentrations of ssDNA, dPCV2 exhibits no cleavage activity, highlighting the critical role of the catalytic tyrosine residue in PCV2 endonuclease’s function. Further exploration using truncated ssDNA oligonucleotides and mutagenesis analysis unveils the intricate relationship between sequence variations and activity, providing insights into substrate recognition and cleavage efficiency. Additionally, our investigation into PCV2-GFP's reactivity with double-stranded DNA (dsDNA) demonstrates a specific preference for ssDNA substrates, underscoring its specialized role in recognizing and cleaving single-stranded nucleic acids. These findings advance our understanding of PCV2 endonucleases, offering valuable insights into their biological significance and potential applications in viral biology.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
